ABSTRACT
Personnel emoluments are typically among the largest recurrent items of public expenditure, and in education salaries account for most of the budget. When payroll records are inaccurate, fragmented, or poorly verified, public money is lost and trust in public financial management declines. Public sector payroll management in Zambia therefore requires robust verification controls, automated validation, and continuous audit mechanisms so that salaries are paid only to authorized, verified employees.
This technical report evaluates the challenges faced by Zambia’s Payroll Management and Establishment Control (PMEC) system, using Auditor General reports and parliamentary disclosures published up to October 2026. The findings include 125 Ministry of Education officers who could not be accounted for at their workstations (K1,228,635), 4 officers absent for more than 12 months (K224,612), 205 underage entries and 108 net-pay threshold breaches in 2024, and 1,524 of 4,319 recruited teachers still awaiting placement in March 2026.
The report places these findings within the literature on transparency and accountability, financial management information systems, international evidence on ghost workers and payroll leakage, continuous auditing, and information security. The literature shows that technology alone does not produce accountability; it works only with clear ownership of records, reliable data, trained staff, and defined follow-up.
Building on this evidence, the report proposes One Payroll Management System, a single integrated platform in which HR records, approved posts, station verification, and payroll operate on one authoritative dataset. The design combines a 5-step workflow (Register, Verify, Validate, Review, Report), a three-layer prototype architecture on a Node.js server and relational database, automated validation rules, exception dashboards, role-based access control, and an immutable audit trail.
Crucially, the controls emphasize procedural fairness: a digital flag opens a case for investigation and never triggers an automatic salary stoppage. The report concludes with four recommendations: standardize staff records, schedule multi-level verifications, track every flagged case to resolution, and pilot the system before national rollout.
Keywords: payroll management, transparency, accountability, establishment control, PMEC, ghost workers, audit trail, role-based access control, Zambia
