2.2 Prolonged Absences & Validation Rule Weaknesses
The 2025 audit also highlighted 4 officers who remained on the payroll despite being reported absent for over 12 months, accumulating K224,612 in payments. Furthermore, historical findings from 2024 revealed 205 underage records and 108 instances where take-home pay fell below the mandated 40% threshold, illustrating that automated software rules must run consistently during processing runs.
2.3 Teacher Recruitment & Placement Bottlenecks
Parliamentary records from 17 March 2026 regarding the July 2025 teacher replacement exercise indicated that of 4,319 recruited teachers, only 2,795 (64.7%) had been placed on the active payroll at that date, leaving 1,524 pending placement (National Assembly, 2026). This highlights significant manual processing backlogs during high-volume recruitments.
Chapter 2 continues beyond the Zambian audit evidence to review the conceptual and empirical literature that informs the design of the proposed system.
2.4 Transparency and Accountability in Public Financial Management
Transparency and accountability are often mentioned together but are conceptually distinct. Bovens (2007) describes accountability as a relationship in which an actor has an obligation to explain and justify conduct to a forum, which can question the actor, pass judgement, and impose consequences. Payroll is a clear example: officers who authorize, verify, and process salaries must be able to justify their actions to supervisors, auditors, and Parliament. In Zambia, the Public Finance Management Act No. 1 of 2018 provides the statutory framework for public finances, and controls over personal-emolument expenditure operate within it (Republic of Zambia, 2018).
Fox (2007) cautions that transparency does not automatically produce accountability. He distinguishes “soft” accountability, which is answerability through information and justification, from “hard” accountability, which involves sanctions or remedies, and he separates “clear” transparency, which reveals who is responsible and what was decided, from “opaque” transparency, which discloses information without showing responsibility. Meijer (2013) likewise treats transparency as a complex social process rather than a simple release of data, and Hood (2007) notes that greater transparency can encourage blame-avoidance behaviour among officials. The implication for payroll design is that publishing figures is not enough. A system must record who changed what, when, and under whose authority, and it must attach case ownership and deadlines to every exception; these are the features proposed in Chapter 3.
2.5 Payroll and Financial Management Information Systems in Developing Countries
Payroll systems form part of the broader family of financial management information systems (FMIS). Diamond and Khemani (2005) reviewed the introduction of FMIS in developing countries and found that results were often below expectations, largely because the systems were treated as technical fixes without the underlying process reform, political commitment, and realistic scoping that they require. The World Bank’s review of 25 years of FMIS experience reaches a similar conclusion: technology is an enabler rather than a substitute for institutional reform, and lasting results depend on government ownership, phased implementation, simple designs, and good-quality data (Dener et al., 2011).
Heeks (2002) explains many failures of information systems in developing countries through “design–reality gaps”, where the assumptions built into a system differ from the skills, infrastructure, and working practices of the organization that must use it. This is a particular risk for a payroll system that depends on district and school officers entering accurate data. Earlier work on IT-enabled public sector reform and e-government also stresses that benefits such as reduced discretion, faster processing, and greater openness are realized only when the technology is matched to administrative reform (Bhatnagar, 2004; Heeks, 1999, 2006). The World Bank (2016) makes the same point for digital technologies generally, arguing that their benefits depend on “analog complements” such as regulation, skills, and accountable institutions.
Margetts and Dunleavy (2013) describe a second wave of digital-era governance in which governments re-integrate fragmented functions and redesign administrative processes around digital data. This supports the single-system principle adopted in this report: where human-resource, establishment, and payroll data are held separately and reconciled manually, mismatches are expected, whereas a shared authoritative record reduces the opportunities for them to arise.
2.6 Ghost Workers, Absenteeism and Payroll Leakage: International Evidence
The problems seen in the Zambian audit reports are not unique to Zambia. Chaudhury et al. (2006) conducted unannounced visits to schools and health facilities in six developing countries and found that, on average, about one in five teachers and about one in three health workers were absent from their posts. Muralidharan et al. (2017) studied teacher absence in India and estimated its fiscal cost at roughly US$1.5 billion per year, arguing that weak governance and monitoring have a direct and measurable cost to the public purse.
Reinikka and Svensson (2004) examined a capitation grant in Uganda and found that schools received only a small share of the funds intended for them. They also found that a public information campaign, which let schools and parents see what was being transferred, significantly improved the flow of funds. Together, these studies show that absence from post and leakage of funds are measurable governance problems, and that they respond to better information, monitoring, and local verification. They are consistent with the Zambian findings on officers who could not be accounted for at their stations.
The literature also supports the due-process position taken in this report. Absence from a workstation has many causes, including approved leave, illness, study, transfer, and delayed record updates, as well as genuine fraud. A system that automatically stopped salaries on the basis of a flag would risk penalizing staff for recording errors. Flags should therefore start an investigation, not end one.
2.7 Integrated Design, Reconciliation and Continuous Auditing
Audit reports normally reach Parliament long after the transactions they describe. The 2025 findings discussed in Section 2.1, for example, concern payments made over a prior financial year. Vasarhelyi and Halper (1991) proposed continuous auditing, in which automated tests are applied to transactions as they occur rather than being reviewed once a year. Applied to payroll, the rules that failed in the 2024 audit (age bounds and the 40% net-pay threshold) would run in every payroll cycle, and exceptions would be raised before payment rather than after.
International auditing standards also emphasize the importance of reliable, traceable evidence. The INTOSAI fundamental principles of public-sector auditing (ISSAI 100) set out the expectations for auditors in the public sector (INTOSAI, 2019), and the OECD Recommendation on Public Integrity highlights risk-based internal control and transparency as parts of an integrity system (OECD, 2017). A payroll platform that keeps a complete, time-stamped history of changes gives internal and external auditors the evidence they need and reduces the time spent on manual reconstruction of records. Reconciliation between human-resource registers and PMEC extracts, as proposed in Section 3.2, supports the same aim by identifying mismatches each month rather than annually.
2.8 Security, Access Control and Audit Trails
A payroll system holds sensitive personal and financial data and must therefore be designed with security as a core requirement. Saltzer and Schroeder (1975) set out design principles that remain widely used, including least privilege, under which each user receives only the access needed for their duties. Sandhu et al. (1996) formalized role-based access control (RBAC), in which permissions are assigned to roles and users are assigned to roles. RBAC suits payroll because job functions such as data entry, verification, approval, and audit are clearly defined and can be kept separate, which supports separation of duties.
Accountability also depends on trustworthy records of what happened. Schneier and Kelsey (1999) describe secure audit logs that are designed so that an attacker, or an insider, cannot undetectably alter earlier entries even after gaining control of the system. This is the principle behind the immutable audit trail proposed in this report. Organizations can structure their security management using ISO/IEC 27001:2022, which sets requirements for an information security management system (ISO/IEC, 2022). Because payroll data identifies individuals and their earnings, it is also personal data, and its processing in Zambia is subject to the Data Protection Act No. 3 of 2021, which makes collection limits, security safeguards, and lawful processing relevant to system design (Republic of Zambia, 2021).
2.9 Adoption and Success of Public-Sector Information Systems
Even a well-designed system fails if staff do not use it properly. Davis (1989) showed that perceived usefulness and perceived ease of use are strong predictors of whether users accept information technology. DeLone and McLean (2003) proposed that information system success should be assessed across system quality, information quality, service quality, use, user satisfaction, and net benefits. Heeks (2002) adds that public-sector projects in developing countries commonly fail in whole or in part when these human and organizational factors are neglected.
These frameworks inform the proposed evaluation of the system. Useful indicators include the share of staff records matched to a verified station, the time taken to resolve flagged cases, the number of open exceptions, and the extent to which district officers complete scheduled verifications. They also support the recommendation to pilot the system in selected districts before national rollout.
2.10 Summary of the Literature and Research Gap
The literature establishes three points. First, accountability requires answerability and consequences, not just disclosure (Bovens, 2007; Fox, 2007). Second, international evidence shows that absence from post and leakage of funds are widespread and measurable, and that monitoring and information improve results (Chaudhury et al., 2006; Reinikka & Svensson, 2004). Third, FMIS and e-government experience shows that technology succeeds only with institutional reform, good data, and attention to users (Dener et al., 2011; Diamond & Khemani, 2005; Heeks, 2002).
Published work that connects these ideas to Zambian audit findings in a single payroll control design that also protects employee due process appears limited. This report addresses that gap by translating the documented PMEC findings into an integrated workflow, architecture, and set of safeguards.
