CHAPTER 3 – METHODOLOGY & TECHNICAL ARCHITECTURE
3.1 Proposed 5-Step Digital Control Workflow
To systematically prevent payroll mismatches, a 5-step digital verification workflow is proposed:
1.	Register: Maintain a centralized register with unique Staff IDs, station assignments, and employment status.
2.	Verify: Authorized district officers conduct scheduled physical and digital workstation verifications.
3.	Validate: Automated rules engines verify records against duplicate check constraints, age bounds, and net-pay rules.
4.	Review: System flags trigger manual officer investigations to collect evidence before administrative decisions.
5.	Report: Interactive dashboards monitor open exception cases, turnaround times, and completed actions.
3.2 Prototype System Architecture
A prototype architecture built on a Node.js server, relational database, and secure browser interface allows decentralized verification at school and district levels (Figure 3.1).
The platform incorporates core modules including Transfer Tracking (maintaining historical transfer logs), Reconciliation Engines (comparing active HR registers with PMEC extracts), and Immutable Audit Trails. Verification officers interact with the system through a secure browser interface, while the automated validation engine and exception dashboard support the Validate, Review, and Report steps of the workflow in Section 3.1.
 
Figure 3.1: Prototype Technical Architecture & Module Data Flow
3.3 Automated Validation Rules
The Validate step applies a set of rules to every record in each payroll cycle. Each rule answers a control weakness documented in Chapter 2. A failed rule creates a case for review; it never stops a salary automatically (Table 3.1).
Table 3.1: Proposed Automated Validation Rules
Rule	Check Performed	Audit Finding Addressed
Unique identity	One Staff ID per person; duplicate IDs, national registration numbers, or bank accounts are flagged.	Duplicate and ghost entries (Section 2.1)
Age bounds	Date of birth must give an age within the permitted employment range.	205 underage entries (2024)
Net-pay threshold	Take-home pay must not fall below 40% of pay.	108 threshold breaches (2024)
Station match	Each active employee is linked to an approved post and a verified station.	125 officers unaccounted for (2025)
Absence tracking	Absence beyond a set period raises a case until the status is updated.	4 officers absent > 12 months (2025)
Placement ageing	Recruits not placed on the payroll within a set period are listed for follow-up.	1,524 teachers pending (2026)
3.4 Roles and Access Control
Access follows the least-privilege and separation-of-duties principles reviewed in Section 2.8. Each role sees and changes only what its duties require, and no one can approve a case that they raised themselves (Table 3.2).
Table 3.2: Proposed User Roles and Permissions
Role	Main Permissions	Key Restriction
Station officer	Enters and updates station returns and staff status.	Own station only
District verifier	Records physical and digital verification results and evidence.	Cannot approve own cases
Case reviewer	Investigates flagged cases and records the resolution.	Cannot change pay data
Payroll officer	Applies approved changes to payroll records.	Approved cases only
Auditor	Reads records, cases, and the audit trail.	Read-only access
System administrator	Manages user accounts and roles.	No approval of payroll changes
3.5 Operating Cycle and Implementation Considerations
The system runs on a monthly cycle aligned with payroll processing. Stations submit digital returns, the reconciliation engine compares the HR register with the PMEC extract, the validation rules run on the combined data, and flagged records are routed to case reviewers with a resolution deadline. Dashboards then report open cases, turnaround times, and completed actions to district, provincial, and national supervisors.
Because many schools have limited connectivity, the browser interface should be lightweight and tolerate slow connections, with district offices acting as the main points of data entry where school access is unreliable. Training for station and district officers is essential, since design–reality gaps and low user acceptance are leading causes of failure (Davis, 1989; Heeks, 2002).
Existing records should be cleaned and matched to unique Staff IDs before go-live; otherwise poor-quality legacy data would be carried into the new system (Dener et al., 2011).
CHAPTER 4 – RESULTS & DISCUSSION
Analysis indicates that payroll discrepancies in Zambia stem primarily from record mismatches between local station returns and central database entries, delayed multi-step recruitment approvals, and high manual workloads during recruitment drives. Addressing these requires combining technical validation with strict governance safeguards.
4.1 Risk Analysis & Systemic Gaps
The documented findings in Chapter 2 map onto three systemic gaps. First, record mismatches between station returns and central entries are consistent with the 125 officers who could not be accounted for across 24 stations and the 4 officers who remained on the payroll after more than 12 months of reported absence. Second, delayed multi-step approvals are reflected in the 1,524 of 4,319 recruited teachers (35.3%) still pending placement on the payroll at 17 March 2026. Third, high manual workloads during recruitment drives are consistent with the 205 underage entries and 108 net-pay threshold breaches recorded in the 2024 audit, which PMEC attributed to human error (News Diggers, 2025).
These findings describe control weaknesses in record-keeping and verification. An officer who cannot be accounted for at a workstation is a verification outcome that requires investigation; it is not by itself a determination of wrongdoing. The proposed workflow in Section 3.1 is designed accordingly.
4.2 Security, Fairness & Data Protection
The proposed controls are designed so that technology supports, rather than replaces, administrative judgement. A flag raised by the validation engine opens a case for manual investigation and evidence collection; it does not trigger an automatic salary stoppage. This protects employee due process and reduces the risk of penalizing staff for data-entry or reporting errors.
Role-based access control limits each user to the records and actions their duties require, and an immutable audit trail records who viewed or changed a record and when. Every flagged mismatch is assigned a case number, a reviewer, and a resolution deadline so that cases remain traceable. Because the platform holds personal and salary information, it should collect only the data needed for verification and should be operated in line with applicable Zambian data protection requirements.
CHAPTER 5 – CONCLUSION & RECOMMENDATIONS
Digital technology can substantially strengthen public payroll accuracy and transparency in Zambia. However, software rules must be paired with clear operational ownership, trained personnel, and rigid follow-up procedures.
